---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import { CardGrid, LinkCard } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import ChunkList from '../../components/ChunkList.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/data';
import { byEnglish, byVerb, coll, collChunkUrl, collTopics } from '../../lib/collocations';

const headings = [
	{ depth: 2, slug: 'stages', text: 'Stages' },
	{ depth: 2, slug: 'browse', text: 'Browse' },
	{ depth: 2, slug: 'topics', text: 'Topics' },
	...collTopics.map((t) => ({ depth: 3, slug: t.key, text: t.name })),
	{ depth: 2, slug: 'reading-an-entry', text: 'Reading an entry' },
];
const shared = byVerb.filter((g) => g.items.length > 1).length;
const traps = byEnglish.filter((g) => g.items.length > 1).length;
---

{/* The collocation course is separate from the main list's; it starts here rather than continuing from it. */}
<StarlightPage frontmatter={{ title: 'Collocations', description: 'The noun and verb sets Japanese speakers use together all the time, chosen by how common and typical they are.', prev: false }} headings={headings}>
	<p class="vl-lead">
		The noun and verb sets Japanese speakers use together all the time: rain falls (<i translate="no">ame ga furu</i>),
		open the door (<i translate="no">doa o akeru</i>), catch a cold (<i translate="no">kaze o hiku</i>).
	</p>
	<p class="vl-count">
		{coll.stats.total} sets in {coll.stats.chunks} chunks, chosen by how often they’re said and how typical the pairing is,
		and ordered into four stages that run alongside the main list’s stages. Each chunk is a short page; use Next at the
		bottom of each page.
	</p>

	<h2 id="stages">Stages</h2>
	<CardGrid>
		{coll.stages.map((s, i) => (
			<LinkCard title={`${s.name} · ${s.items} collocations`} description={s.summary} href={url(`collocations/${i + 1}/`)} />
		))}
	</CardGrid>

	<h2 id="browse">Browse</h2>
	<CardGrid>
		<LinkCard title="By verb" description={`Each Japanese verb with the nouns it pairs with. ${shared} verbs pair with more than one noun.`} href={url('collocations/verbs/')} />
		<LinkCard title="By English word" description={`The English word you’d reach for, and the Japanese verb each noun really takes. ${traps} English words split into several.`} href={url('collocations/english/')} />
		<LinkCard title="Cuts" description={`${coll.cuts.length} candidates that didn’t make the list, with the reason for each.`} href={url('collocations/cuts/')} />
	</CardGrid>

	<h2 id="topics">Topics</h2>
	{collTopics.map((t) => (
		<>
			<h3 id={t.key}>{t.name}</h3>
			<ChunkList blocks={t.blocks} showCategory hrefOf={collChunkUrl} chipOf={(b) => `Stage ${b.stage}`} />
		</>
	))}

	<h2 id="reading-an-entry">Reading an entry</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><b>The headword</b> is the plain form of the whole set (<i translate="no">ame ga furu</i>); <i>polite</i> is its desu/masu form. The examples use the form people actually say, often without <i translate="no">ga</i> or <i translate="no">o</i> (<i translate="no">ame futteru</i>).</li>
		<li><b>194×</b> is how many times the set was heard in 3.17 million lines of Japanese film and TV subtitles. A dash means it was too rare in films to count; those sets, mostly Japanese daily life the films don’t show, were chosen on a native speaker’s judgement.</li>
		<li><b>Typical 52% of ame</b> means that of everything said with <i translate="no">ame</i>, 52% goes with this verb. The higher it is, the more the two words belong together.</li>
		<li><b>Verb</b> or <b>adjective</b> shows the partner on its own and what it means. <b>Trap</b>, when there is one, is the mistake English leads you to.</li>
		<li><b>Main list</b> links the versatility-list words the set builds on, with the stage they’re taught in.</li>
	</ul>
</StarlightPage>
